  "Number": 15,
  "Question": "The root(s) of \\(\\dfrac{15}{x^2-4}-\\dfrac2{x-2}=1\\) is (are):",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-5\\) and \\(3\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\pm2\\)",
    "C": "\\(2\\) only",
    "D": "\\(-3\\) and \\(5\\)",
    "E": "\\(3\\) only"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "First factor \\(x^2-4=(x-2)(x+2)\\). The original equation is defined only for \\(x\\ne2,-2\\). On this domain, multiplying by the nonzero expression \\(x^2-4\\) clears both denominators:\n\\[\n15-2(x+2)=x^2-4.\n\\]\nExpanding and collecting terms gives\n\\[\nx^2+2x-15=0.\n\\]\nThe numbers \\(5\\) and \\(-3\\) have sum \\(2\\) and product \\(-15\\), so\n\\[\n(x+5)(x-3)=0.\n\\]\nThus \\(x=-5\\) or \\(x=3\\). Neither makes an original denominator zero. Since clearing the denominators was reversible on the allowed domain, both are valid and there are no other roots. The roots are \\(-5\\) and \\(3\\), choice A.",
